keywords: ["cellular automata", "probabilistic cellular automata", "noise", "ergodicity", "undecidability", "halting problem", "Gibbs measures", "spontaneous magnetization", "noise diagram", "phase diagrams", "exponential weak mixing", "effectively open sets"]
abstract: "Which sets of noise rates or inverse temperatures can separate rapid forgetting from persistent order? We construct one finite-range cellular automaton on the line with computable disjoint rational noise windows: throughout a program's window it is uniformly exponentially ergodic if the program halts, whereas a one-site clock fails to converge from a specified initial law otherwise. The same dichotomy holds at one fixed positive rational rate for a computable family of radius-one rules. On the square lattice, one finite-energy interaction has arbitrary-volume exponential weak spatial mixing in halting windows and opposite translation-invariant Gibbs states in nonhalting windows. We then study the full mixing set of finite rational magnets in a positive rational inverse-temperature window. Every such set is relatively effectively open. Conversely, every effectively open set is the union of nested actual mixing sets of a computable sequence of magnets with a common alphabet; every stage has opposite ordered states outside the target set, and the spatial range of the stages may grow. For every explicitly specified rational semialgebraic open diagram in an exponential temperature coordinate, one finite nearest-neighbor magnet has exactly that mixing set and is ordered everywhere else in the window. At most three hidden line states per direction suffice, and three can be necessary for ferromagnetic 65536-color Potts line decorations with identical matrices in both directions and exact periodic Perron weights. Whether one finite magnet realizes every effectively open set remains open."
